rsions of the base model. Empirically speaking, fine-tuning should not change head norms ‖ ω ‖ 2 \|\omega\|_{2} by much, and as such the feature alignment becomes the important term. Analogously, Equation 4 demonstrates that when the teacher features are equally (mis-)aligned with the student features, entropy becomes the useful router. Indeed, in the Triton-thinking task, neither teacher is from the same model family as the base model, so the student and the teachers naturally do not have much feature alignment. In that sense, we would expect the entropy-based routing to be more effective, which it indeed is. 6 Conclusion In this work, we explore token-level teacher routing as an additional axis for OPD training. To that end, we propose a theoretical framework in the log-linear setting to unify existing classes of teacher selection methods (e.g. entropy-based) as well as a new class of representation-based routing that we propose. Together, these allow us to predict empirically when one scheme may be more beneficial than the other, in the context of the trained student’s ability and output diversity. We believe there is room for much more design and improvement on this axis, as well as understanding of its downstream benefits. For instance, an interesting open question would be to understand if the diversity improvements of our teacher selection scheme can be used to create a better warm start for RL post-OPD. In addition, it would also be worthwhile to derive teacher selection metrics that are more reward-aware, beyond simply choosing the strongest teacher. AI use statement In this work, we used generative AI tools for literature search, writing code, and brainstorming ideas. We have reviewed all AI-assisted work. We take responsibility for the final content of this work, including text, claims or artifacts produced with the aid of generative AI. References Agarwal et al. (2024) Rishabh Agarwal, Nino Vieillard, Yongchao Zhou, Piotr Stanczyk, Sabela Ramos, Matthieu