such as TRPO and PPO [ Schulman et al., 2015 , Schulman et al., 2017 ] , a growing line of theoretical work has been aimed towards understanding what sorts of reward models yield favorable optimization guarantees [ Razin et al., 2025 , Shang et al., 2026 ] . In addition, there has also been an extensive line of work understanding the interplay between on- and off-policy data in training, most notably in contrasting the post-training phases of SFT and RL [ Shenfeld et al., 2025 , Wei and Kim, 2026 , Park et al., 2025 , Matsutani et al., 2026 , Huang et al., 2026 ] . On-policy distillation. On-policy distillation (OPD) replaces the scalar reward in RL with dense token-level supervision from a teacher queried on the student’s own rollouts [ Hinton et al., 2015 , Agarwal et al., 2024 ] . G-OPD connects vanilla OPD to KL-regularized RL and separates the reward scale from the regularization strength [ Yang et al., 2026a ] , and recent work by Li et al. [2026a] finds that OPD followed by RL outperforms RL alone. A related line of work in the direction of OPD is on-policy self-distillation (OPSD), which removes the separate teacher by conditioning the same model on privileged context such as a reference solution [ Zhao et al., 2026 ] , with unsupervised [ Li et al., 2026c ] and shortcut-purified [ Shen et al., 2026 ] variants. In addition, MOPD is a way to distill from several independently trained domain experts [ Ma et al., 2026 ] . OPD dynamics and prefix drift. Several recent works examine why dense teacher feedback can nonetheless be hard to learn from. For instance, Fu et al. [2026a] separate the token-local update from the sequence-level reverse KL gradient and identify imbalanced sampled-token signals and support mismatch as failure modes; Li et al. [2026b] find that compatible thinking patterns and genuinely new capabilities are distinct conditions for transfer; and Fu et al. [2026b] show that a single query already induces broad state visitation while the supervision itself is incorporated slowly. On the theory side, Zhang et al. [2026] show that the advantage of online over offline imitation in post-training hinges on non-realizability of the expert rather than on horizon-driven error accumulation, and Sriraman et al. [2026] show that with noisy expert feedback, OPD attains polynomial rather than exponential-in-horizon sample complexity. Additional analyses have suggested that prefixes are generally off-policy for the teacher: early-token supervision is more effective than late-token supervision [ Xie et al., 2026 ] , teacher confidence decays along student rollouts [ Liu et al., 2026b ] , and student–teacher mismatch invites length-dependent exploitation of the objective [ Wang et al., 2026 ] . Our work provides an additional theoretical perspective for understanding teachers that yield good optimization guarantees under OPD. Teacher selection, student compatibility, and token-level supervision. A more general line of work that spans the knowledge distillation literature is understanding teacher suitability for the student. For example GRACE scores teachers by the distribution of student gradients on their outputs [ Panigrahi et al., 2025 ] , and the Rank–Surprisal Ratio proposed by Yang et al. [2026b] balances alignment with the student against informativeness. Indeed, both of these above works choose a teacher once for the entire training duration. H-OPD [ Yin et al., 2026 ] suggests the use of confidence as a way to do token-level routing, but their analysis is limited to the setting of vision tasks. Some more recent works analyze the other end of the spectrum, which is to see how little supervision one can get away with in OPD while preserving its advantages. For instance, OPSA replaces the teacher advantage on low-probability tokens [ Ding and Zhang, 2026 ] , and Liu et al. [2026c] supervise only one or two tokens per rollout. Our insight then, is that if we specialize to the on-policy training case, then we can naturally extend teacher arbitration to the token level, using different routing schemes depending on the setting. Interventions for better coverage. The coverage principle of Chen et al. [2025] argues that the probability a model already assigns to high-quality responses governs what post-training and test-time selection can achieve. This framework has guided several follow-up works in terms of better interventions for improving coverage. For instance, TailSFT filters already-fit sequences out of SFT so that learning concentrates on under-modeled regions, improving pass@ k k before RL [ Malladi et al., 2026 ] . In addition, Cheng et al. [2026] trace the gap between pass@1 gains and pass@ N N losses after RL to a reshaping of strategy selection and connect it to coverage and transfer. The OPD-then-RL setup of Li et al. [2026a] can also be interpreted as first broadening the set of teacher-supported solutions and then concentrating probability on the successful ones. Moreover, Tuyls et al. [2026] also designs a representation-aware RL post-training scheme in order to induce better diversity of output responses. In our work, we prove that our representation-based teacher selection scheme in Section 4.3 is coverage-aware in a similar spirit; that is, our diversity analysis in Section 4.5 upper bounds the undercoverage quantity of high-quality continuations. Appendix B Proofs of the Feature-Matching Teacher Selection We briefly describe the organization of this section below. First, in Section B.1 , we describe how teacher competence (as measured in expected reward) and teacher-student compatibility complement each other in terms of the training outcome of OPD, via the performance difference lemma; notably, this also allows us to realize the potential of token-level teacher selection over using only a single teacher. Then, in Section B.2 , we list out the properties of the gradient and optimization landscape at initialization to work towards a proof of Theorem 1 . Afterwards, in Section B.3 , we explicitly quantify the benefit of using our feature-matching selection scheme to give the full proof of Theorem 1 . Finally in Section B.4 , we provide the full proofs on how our selection scheme can improve the coverage of good response modes. B.1 Why Minimize Token-Level KL? A Performance-Difference View Recall the performance difference lemma: J ⁡ ( π ⋆ ) − J ⁡ ( π θ ) \displaystyle J(\pi^{\star})-J(\pi_{	heta}) = Δ T ⏟ teacher competence gap + J ⁡ ( π T ) − J ⁡ ( π θ ) ⏟ student-teacher gap \displaystyle=\underbrace{\Delta_{T}}_{	ext{teacher competence gap}}+\underbrace{J(\pi_{T})-J(\pi_{	heta})}_{	ext{student-teacher gap}} = Δ T − ∑ t = 1 H 𝔼 s t ∼ d t π θ , v ∼ π θ ( ⋅ ∣ s t ) A t π T ( s t , v ) \displaystyle=\Delta_{T}-\sum_{t=1}^{H}\mathbb{E}_{s_{t}\sim d_{t}^{\pi_{	heta}},\,v\sim\pi_{	heta}(\cdot\mid s_{t})}A_{t}^{\pi_{T}}(s_{t},v) = Δ T + ∑ t = 1 H 𝔼 s t ∼ d t π θ ​ ∑ v ∈ 𝒱 ( π T ​ ( v ∣ s t ) − π θ ​ ( v ∣ s t ) ) ​ Q t π T ​ ( s t , v ) . \displaystyle=\Delta_{T}+\sum_{t=1}^{H}\mathbb{E}_{s_{t}\sim d_{t}^{\pi_{	heta}}}\sum_{v\in\mathcal{V}} igl(\pi_{T}(v\mid s_{t})-\pi_{	heta}(v\mid s_{t}) igr)Q_{t}^{\pi_{T}}(s_{t},v). (6) We now prove Lemma 2 from the main text. Proof of Lemma 2 . Since the terminal reward lies in [ 0 , 1 ] [0,1] , its conditional expectation Q t π T ​ ( s t , v ) Q_{t}^{\pi_{T}}(s_{t},v) also lies in [ 0 , 1 ] [0,1] for every prefix s t s_{t} and token v v . Moreover, the student and teacher probabilities each sum to one. Thus, for every fixed prefix, | ∑ v ∈ 𝒱 ( π T ​ ( v ∣ s t ) − π θ ​ ( v ∣ s t ) ) ​ Q t π T ​ ( s t , v ) | \displaystyle\left|\sum_{v\in\mathcal{V}} igl(\pi_{T}(v\mid s_{t})-\pi_{	heta}(v\mid s_{t}) igr)Q_{t}^{\pi_{T}}(s_{t},v)ight| = | ∑ v ∈ 𝒱 ( π T ​ ( v ∣ s t ) − π θ ​ ( v ∣ s t ) ) ​ ( Q t π T ​ ( s t , v ) − 1 2 ) | \displaystyle\quad=\left|\sum_{v\in\mathcal{V}} igl(\pi_{T}(v\mid s_{t})-\pi_{	heta}(v\mid s_{t}) igr)\left(Q_{t}^{\pi_{T}}(s_{t},v)-	frac{1}{2}ight)ight| ≤ 1 2 ​ ∑ v ∈ 𝒱 |